  "diagnosis_text": "The h1\u2192h5 transfer is confirmed severely degraded: a 5-second TCP test achieved only **~0.20 Mbit/s** (131 KB received). Connectivity is intact\u201410/10 pings succeeded\u2014but RTT is unusually high and variable (**73\u2013141 ms**, 98.7 ms average).\n\n### Localisation\n\nThe installed forwarding rule sends h1\u2192h5 traffic:\n\n`h1 \u2192 s3 port 1 \u2192 s1 port 4 \u2192 s5 port 3 \u2192 h5`\n\nThe key evidence is on **s1 port 4**, the egress link toward **s5 port 1**:\n\n- Port is enabled and the physical link is active.\n- Measured transmit rate was approximately **1.0 Mbit/s**.\n- Queue backlog was present: **12,112 bytes / 8 packets**.\n- The two-second sample recorded **363 queue overlimits**.\n- No interface drops or errors were reported.\n\nBy comparison, the preceding **s1 port 2 \u2194 s3 port 1** link had no queue buildup, no overlimits, drops, or errors during its sample.\n\n### Diagnosis\n\nThe bottleneck is localised to the **s1 port 4 \u2192 s5 port 1** direction. Its behavior is consistent with a heavily constrained/rate-limited or congested egress queue: the queue is repeatedly over limit and traffic is held near 1 Mbit/s. The link itself is not down and there is no evidence of physical errors.\n\nThe next operational check should be the egress QoS/shaping and queue configuration on **s1 port 4**, plus any competing traffic using that same s1\u2192s5 link.",
